redefine the requested\\nanalysis. When plausible interpretations materially change the result, choose\\nthe interpretation best supported by the task and data before accepting the\\nanswer.\\n\\n### Allowed Galaxy Tools\\n\\nUse ordinary usegalaxy.org tools, workflows, or a fresh user-defined tool\\n(UDT). Among ordinary tools, do not use Jupyter, RStudio, Galaxy Notebook,\\nAWK/gawk, JQ, Query Tabular, SQLite, SQL-like tools, or wrappers that accept\\narbitrary code as the computation payload.\\n\\n### Local Code Allowlist\\n\\n**Before upload:** Inspect metadata, headers, dimensions, and archive members\\nwhen needed for staging; extract unchanged archive members; perform lossless\\nformat conversion only when Galaxy cannot ingest the original format.\\n\\n**After Galaxy:** From downloaded Galaxy outputs only, select rows or columns,\\nsort, apply thresholds to Galaxy-computed values, count rows, calculate simple\\nsummaries or ratios from Galaxy-derived values, round, and format the answer.\\nLocal extraction may read already-computed scalar values or attributes from a\\nGalaxy output, but must not fit, predict, optimize, or perform other scientific\\ncomputation.\\n\\nIf removing all Galaxy steps would still let local code produce the answer,\\nredo the computation on Galaxy.\\n\\n### Route Selection\\n\\nUse ordinary Galaxy tools or workflows, or a fresh UDT. Choose the shortest\\nroute that faithfully implements the method and quantity requested by the\\ntask. Do not silently replace a named method, model, estimator, metric, or data\\nsource with a different one. A search result is only a compact candidate: when\\nit appears relevant, inspect that tool before rejecting the ordinary-tool\\nroute. Use a UDT only when no plausible candidate exists or targeted inspection\\nshows that the required operation is unavailable.\\n\\n### Galaxy Access\\n\\nUse `search_galaxy_tools` with concise tool-family, operation, package, or\\ntarget-metric terms for live ordinary-